DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/the-city-and-the-shadow\n報
 名/票券: https://luma.com/omsel11m\n\nThis talk examines the history of
  state housing in Seoul from the late colonial era through to the beginnin
 g of the high-growth period in the 1960s and 1970s. State housing programs
  in the postcolonial era became a battleground over some of the most press
 ing questions of the day\, ranging from the priorities of postwar reconstr
 uction to the proper shape of South Korean society. This talk focuses in p
 articular on the infamous Citizen Apartments program\, an initiative spear
 headed by Mayor Kim Hyŏnok (in office 1966-1970) that made promises of ho
 using for sŏmin\, or “ordinary” South Koreans.\n\nPlease RSVP here. \
 n\nAbout the speaker:\n\nRussell Burge is Korea Foundation Assistant Profe
 ssor of Korean History in the Department of East Asian Languages and Cultu
 res at Indiana University Bloomington. He holds an A.M. in Regional Studie
 s East Asia from Harvard and a Ph.D. in History from Stanford. He was a Po
 stdoctoral Associate at the Council on East Asian Studies and Lecturer in 
 History at Yale University from 2019-2021.\n\nBurge’s interests include 
 development\, democracy\, and social change in South Korea. He is the auth
 or of The Promised Republic: Developmental Society and Making of Modern Se
 oul\, 1961-1979\, which was published in 2026 by Harvard University Asia C
 enter. His second book project is a study of democratization in the 1980s 
 with a special focus on the local history of Taegu (Daegu). Other research
  interests include the politics of memory and histories of crime and crimi
 nality.
